4. GEOMETRY STRATEGY
This is one of the most important engineering decisions.
OneDraft should not initially attempt to build an entirely new CAD kernel.
Instead, the MVP should maintain its own semantic architectural model and use established computational geometry technology underneath it.
The conceptual stack is:
OneDraft Semantic Model
↓
Geometry Abstraction Layer
↓
Computational Geometry Kernel
↓
2D / 3D Representation
The abstraction layer prevents the entire OneDraft system from becoming permanently dependent on one geometry implementation.

5. GEOMETRY KERNEL
The initial geometry implementation should investigate an open computational geometry kernel such as:
Open CASCADE Technology
or another equivalent robust solid-modeling kernel appropriate to the final licensing requirements.
The geometry abstraction layer should expose operations such as:
create_wall()
create_floor()
create_roof()
create_opening()
create_stair()
create_column()
intersect()
subtract()
union()
section()
project()
measure()
The rest of OneDraft should not need to know the internal kernel implementation.

