18. ARIA INTEGRATION
Aria shall not receive unrestricted access to PostgreSQL.
Instead:
Aria
↓
Command Generator
↓
Command Validator
↓
Project Model API
↓
Database Transaction
The AI therefore operates through a controlled interface.

19. ARIA COMMAND SCHEMA
A command should conceptually resemble:
command_id
project_id
revision_id
operation
target_objects
parameters
constraints_to_preserve
reason
requested_by
confidence
validation_requirements
The command is evaluated before execution.

20. COMMAND EXAMPLE
Customer instruction:
Move the east wall 600 mm east while maintaining the building footprint.
Aria converts this to an operation equivalent to:
MOVE_ELEMENT
Target:
WALL-00427
Displacement:
+600 mm X
Preserve:
EXTERIOR_ENVELOPE
Then the command engine evaluates dependencies.
If the command would violate an immutable constraint, the transaction is not silently committed.

21. AI RESPONSE TYPES
Aria should be able to return more than prose.
The internal response categories should include:
ANSWER
COMMAND
CLARIFICATION
WARNING
VALIDATION_RESULT
DESIGN_PROPOSAL
ERROR
REVIEW_REQUIRED
This allows the UI to respond appropriately.

22. MODEL TRANSACTION
A model modification should follow:
BEGIN TRANSACTION
↓
Load current project state
↓
Validate requested command
↓
Apply change
↓
Update dependencies
↓
Regenerate affected geometry
↓
Run required validation
↓
Run drawing impact analysis
↓
Commit or reject
↓
Create revision
↓
Create audit event
This is the core transactional behavior of OneDraft.

23. DEPENDENCY ENGINE
The dependency engine determines what must change after a model modification.
Example:
Wall moved
↓
Room boundary changed
↓
Room area changed
↓
Door position changed
↓
Dimension changed
↓
Section intersection changed
↓
Elevation changed
↓
Drawing affected
Only affected outputs should be regenerated where possible.

